 ## Overview
-ClipGS is a Gaussian Splatting-based framework designed for the real-time interactive cinematic visualization of volumetric medical data with support for dynamic clipping planes [S1]. It solves the problem of maintaining rendering quality and continuity when a user interactsively clips a 3D volume, addressing the artifacts caused by hard visibility truncations and the high computational cost of learning full deformation fields for the additional clipping dimension [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [WildGaussians](6064-wildgaussians.md) or [Dynamic and Temporal Modeling](9265-dynamic-and-temporal-modeling.md) by treating the clipping plane not as a temporal dimension requiring a dense deformation field, but as a geometric visibility constraint managed via a learnable truncation scheme and an Adaptive Adjustment Model (AAM) [S1].
+ClipGS is a Gaussian Splatting-based framework designed for the real-time interactive cinematic visualization of volumetric medical data with support for dynamic clipping planes [S1]. It solves the problem of maintaining rendering quality and continuity when a user interactively clips a 3D volume, addressing the artifacts caused by hard visibility truncations and the high computational cost of learning full deformation fields for the additional clipping dimension [S1]. It addresses the challenge of the introduced dimension transferring the 3D representation to a 4D representation problem, avoiding the high model capacity required by methods that learn a dense deformation field along the clipping dimension by instead modeling clipping plane interaction as a geometric visibility constraint managed via a learnable truncation scheme and an Adaptive Adjustment Model (AAM) [S1].
 
